Generational (nursery) garbage collector
=========================================

Goal: make ANSI-test / large loads fast by not re-marking the large
persistent live set on every collection. Tracked on the `new-gc` branch
(forked from v1.0.0).


WHY (the diagnosis behind this branch)
--------------------------------------

bocl's collector (sources/memory.c) is a non-moving mark-and-sweep with
a flat, per-zone first-fit free list. Loading the ansi-test suite is
super-linear. Measured root causes, in order of discovery:

1. Hash tables were O(n) (alist-backed)        -> fixed pre-1.0 (Slice 166, O(1) buckets).
2. large_list accumulated sub-128B split        -> fixed pre-1.0 (Slice 167, re-file to small_list).
   remainders, so allocation first-fit-walked
   past them (~80% CPU in before_best_free_block).
3. THE REMAINING ISSUE (this branch):
   * The load is ~99% garbage: one collection freed 189 of 191 MB
     (instrument garbage_collect by summing zone sizes; `live` ~ 2 MB).
   * But GC only fires when EVERY zone is full (allocate_in_zones == 0),
     so the heap balloons to ~5 GB / ~72 zones before anything is
     reclaimed; the per-allocation zone walk then scales with the zone
     count -> super-linear load (~24 min, peak RSS ~5 GB).
   * Forcing collection proactively (every ~96 MB) keeps the heap small
     (~196 MB) BUT is ~70x slower (data-and-control-flow module: 215 s
     vs 3 s) -- because the collector is NON-GENERATIONAL, so every
     collection re-marks the large persistent live set (the ~8500-line
     prelude + the ansi-test harness/`*universe*`). Frequent GC == constant
     re-marking of the old generation.

So there is a fundamental tension with the current design:
  rare GC  -> heap balloons -> slow allocation walk;
  frequent GC -> re-marks the persistent set -> slow.
The fix is to stop re-marking the old generation on every collection.


DESIGN (non-moving generational mark-sweep, "sticky mark bits")
---------------------------------------------------------------

bocl is non-moving, so we keep objects in place and track a per-object
generation rather than copying survivors out of a nursery region.

Two generations: 0 = young (nursery), 1 = old.
  * New allocations are gen-0.
  * A MINOR collection marks only gen-0 objects reachable from the roots
    AND from the remembered set; it sweeps only gen-0 garbage. gen-1
    objects are neither traced nor reclaimed -> the persistent set is not
    re-marked. Surviving gen-0 objects are promoted to gen-1 in place.
  * A MAJOR collection is the current full mark+sweep (clear all marks,
    trace everything, sweep all). Run it occasionally (e.g. when the old
    generation has grown by some factor, or every N minor GCs) to reclaim
    dead old objects and cycles between old objects.

Remembered set / write barrier (the crux):
  A gen-0 object reachable only through a gen-1 object would be missed by
  a minor GC that does not trace gen-1. So every store of a Ref into an
  ALREADY-OLD object must be recorded. Options, simplest first:
    (a) Card marking: a byte table, one entry per ~512 B card. The barrier
        marks the card of the mutated slot dirty. Minor GC scans dirty
        cards for old->young pointers. Cheap, coarse, no per-object set.
    (b) Explicit remembered set: a growable array of old objects (or slots)
        that hold young refs; barrier appends, minor GC scans + clears.
  Card marking (a) is recommended -- fewer moving parts, bounded size.

Where the barrier must live: every Ref store into a heap object --
cons_rplaca/rplacd, svref_set, structure/instance slot set, symbol
value/function/plist set, the hash-table bucket writes (hash_table.c),
vector-push, (setf gethash), array (setf aref), etc. Audit for a
chokepoint; if none exists, introduce one (a STORE_REF(obj, slot, val)
macro) and route mutations through it. This audit is step 1 and is the
main risk surface.


IMPLEMENTATION PLAN (incremental, each step validated)
------------------------------------------------------

0. Instrumentation first: land the BOCL_GCLOG hook (freed / heap / zones
   / live per collection) used in the diagnosis, plus minor/major counts.
1. Generation bit: reserve a bit in Block.objcount (currently high bit =
   mark, FREE_BLOCK = all-ones). Pick the next bit down for `old`; adjust
   mark/clear/free/objcount accessors and verify the FREE_BLOCK and
   marked_block_p invariants still hold (these are subtle -- see the
   comments in marked_block_p). Validate with BOCL_HEAPCHECK.
2. Write-barrier chokepoint: route every Ref-into-heap store through one
   macro; card table + dirty-card marking in that macro.
3. Minor collection: mark from roots + dirty cards into gen-0 only; sweep
   gen-0; promote survivors to gen-1; clear dirty cards.
4. Major collection: keep current full GC; trigger heuristically.
5. Collection policy in allocate_internal: minor GC on a young-allocation
   threshold (keeps the heap small) + major GC occasionally. Re-measure
   the ansi-test module load (target: small heap AND fast).

VALIDATION (every step)
-----------------------
* Full SACLA must-* sweep (15 files) must stay crash/hang-free and
  byte-identical to v1.0.0 (errs/fails per file unchanged).
* BOCL_GCSTRESS=N (force GC every N allocs) + BOCL_GCCHECK (survivor ->
  doomed ref check) + BOCL_HEAPCHECK (block/free-list walk) -- a
  generational barrier bug shows up here as a survivor referencing a
  swept young object. See memory note bocl-gc-rooting.
* ansi-test per-module load timing (the /tmp/modtime.lisp driver): the
  late modules (types-and-classes, strings) must drop from minutes to
  seconds while peak RSS stays bounded.

RISK: this touches the core collector + every mutation site. A missed
write barrier = a live young object swept = use-after-free, heap-state
dependent (the exact class documented in bocl-gc-rooting). Go slow; lean
on GCSTRESS/GCCHECK/HEAPCHECK after every step.
